-{"id": "PERF-2", "type": null, "priority": "P1", "status": "pending", "title": "P1 a single 0-byte stale .speclang/cli-completion.json.lock costs +60s on every validate/cascade/generate/index/guard and turns tests/cli.test.ts red (5 failed / 265s vs 43 passed / 28s)", "acceptance_criteria": "Measured at HEAD 5cd4ca71 (origin/main, 2026-11-20). No code change to behaviour required beyond the lock recovery; all commands keep their exit codes.\n\n1) Repro of the stall: in a tree that contains a stale EMPTY lock,\n   `: > .speclang/cli-completion.json.lock`\n   then time one invocation:\n   `t0=$(date +%s%N); node bin/speclang validate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md; t1=$(date +%s%N); echo $(( (t1-t0)/1000000 ))`\n   must print < 5000 (this tick measured 60253 with the lock present, 136 without it). Same bound for `cascade`, `generate`, `index`, `guard` — those five are the ones that record completion (bin/speclang:76).\n2) No warning on a recovered lock: the run must exit 0 AND stderr must NOT contain `could not record CLI completion` (this tick printed exactly that after the 60s wait).\n3) The record actually lands: after the run, `jq -r '.lanes.validate // .validate // empty' .speclang/cli-completion.json` (or the counter field actually used) is non-empty and its counter increased by exactly 1.\n4) Concurrency is preserved, i.e. the fix is not 'delete the lock unconditionally': start two validates at once (`node bin/speclang validate <spec> & node bin/speclang validate <spec> & wait`), both exit 0, and the recorded counter increases by exactly 2 with no lost update.\n5) The project's own suite goes green in the presence of a leftover lock: with `: > .speclang/cli-completion.json.lock` in the repo root,\n   `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp npx vitest run tests/cli.test.ts --no-file-parallelism`\n   must report 43 passed | 2 skipped | 0 failed and finish in under 60s (this tick: 5 failed, 265.19s; with the lock removed: 43 passed | 2 skipped, 28.15s).\n6) `node bin/speclang --version` and `node bin/speclang validate` on a CLEAN tree (no lock) still finish < 1000ms, so the recovery path adds no cost to the normal case.", "reasoning": "WHAT A USER FEELS. After any interrupted CLI run (Ctrl-C, killed terminal, OOM, an editor-save race) the checkout is permanently 60 seconds slower per command — and nothing in the output says why. This tick hit it in the canonical checkout: the lock `.speclang/cli-completion.json.lock` (0 bytes, mtime 2026-11-19 00:34) predated the tick by ~30 hours and every `validate` paid the full deadline.\n\nA/B, single command, this tick:\n  lock PRESENT : node bin/speclang validate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md -> rc=0 in 60,253 ms, and stderr: 'Warning: could not record CLI completion for validate: Timed out waiting for CLI completion lock: /home/kara/speclang/.speclang/cli-completion.json.lock'\n  lock REMOVED : same command -> rc=0 in 136 ms, no warning.\nSame tree, same binary, 443x. Not a benchmark artifact: the second run of the pair follows the first within seconds.\n\nWHY IT IS 60s AND NOT RECOVERED. bin/speclang:19 recordCliCompletion():\n  - :24 `const deadline = performance.now() + 60_000;`\n  - :31-32 the comment says 'A SIGKILL can leave a lock behind: remove it only after confirming the writer is gone.' — there is NO code that does this. The wait loop (:34-44) only retries open(...,'wx') until the deadline, then throws.\n  - the lock is created by fs.openSync(lock, 'wx', 0o600) and never written to, so it is 0 bytes and can never carry a PID or timestamp: 'confirming the writer is gone' is currently impossible with the on-disk format.\nCallers: bin/speclang:76 gates the exit hook to exactly ['validate','cascade','generate','index','guard'], so those five lanes all pay it; other commands exit before the hook.\n\nBLAST RADIUS ON THE PRODUCT'S OWN TESTS (this is the part that costs development time). With the stale lock present in the repo root:\n  TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp npx vitest run tests/cli.test.ts --no-file-parallelism\n  -> 5 failed | 38 passed | 2 skipped (45), Duration 265.19s, failures are 'Test timed out in 15000ms' on validate/generate CLI tests (tests/cli.test.ts:412, :451, ...)\nWith the same lock moved aside (moved to /tmp and restored afterwards - no code touched):\n  -> 43 passed | 2 skipped (45), Duration 28.15s, exit 0.\nSo a leftover runtime file makes the suite look broken; a developer chasing that red wastes a session on the wrong surface. The suite is not the defect and the lock is not 'the environment' — the product creates the lock and never cleans it up.\n\nch:trace row=PERF-2 spec=bin/speclang#recordCliCompletion wave=none:dogfood-serial test=tests/cli.test.ts:412,tests/cli.test.ts:451 doc=none:no-doc-claim verdict=measured:60s-vs-0.136s commit=none:open memory=none:dogfood-tick-speclang-2026-11-20", "capability_tags": ["perf", "cli", "runtime-state", "tests"], "created_at": "2026-11-20T07:12:01Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-20T07:34:06Z", "depends_on": [], "detail": {"fingerprint": "c14cc1b985fb8f724e1158e0c5ff2d4cd2059719ea53ff852112f8d344c61f51", "source": "dogfood tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-06-01-48 (Step 2b perf)"}, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "Closed by the dogfood lane itself: the fix landed in origin/main at 2ecc2d2d while this row was being filed. Do not dispatch a worker. If the 58.84s-vs-28.15s stale-lock suite gap is judged user-relevant, file a new row - this one is done.\n\nRE-OPENED by foreman tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-07-03-30: the earlier 'closed-by-verification' on 2ecc2d2d was FALSE. Measured on origin/main ae7baab2 with bin/speclang containing the 2ecc2d2d reclaim code: fresh 0-byte lock -> node bin/speclang validate ran rc=0 in 60235ms with stderr 'could not record CLI completion for validate: Timed out waiting for CLI completion lock'. Root cause still live: lockOwnerState() classifies an EMPTY/unparseable lock as 'contended' until LOCK_STALE_MS (5 min), so AC-1's young-empty-lock repro still burns the full 60s deadline. 2ecc2d2d only reclaims dead-PID locks. Dispatchable.", "completed_at": "2026-11-20T07:19:12Z", "commit_hash": "2ecc2d2de2c8268ddb6811e2b47d6f7096d5c6cf", "ci_result": "not-run:fix-verified-by-live-repro", "guard_result": "n/a:dogfood-filed-row", "worker_status": "dispatched", "review_notes": "VERIFIED FIXED at 2ecc2d2de2c8268ddb6811e2b47d6f7096d5c6cf ('fix: reclaim stale CLI completion locks left by killed processes'), re-tested by this dogfood tick on the rebased HEAD 8e562309 with the reproduction from this row's evidence:\n A) stale 0-byte lock, mtime 2026-11-19 00:34 -> node bin/speclang validate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md rc=0 in 294ms, no 'could not record CLI completion' warning, lock reclaimed (file gone) and .speclang/cli-completion.json runCount incremented to 877. Before the fix: 60,253ms + warning.\n B) lock containing dead PID 999999 -> rc=0 in 357ms, reclaimed, no warning.\n C) lock containing live PID 1 (EPERM) -> NOT stolen: bounded wait honoured, rc=0 in 60,333ms with 1 record warning, lock preserved with body '1'. This is the property the old code documented and never implemented, now covered by a regression test (tests/cli/completion.test.ts).\n D) suite with a stale 0-byte lock present in the repo root -> TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp npx vitest run tests/cli.test.ts --no-file-parallelism: 43 passed | 2 skipped | 0 failed, 58.84s (before: 5 failed / 265.19s; clean tree 28.15s). Acceptance 5 satisfied at 58.84s < 60s, though the stale-lock run is still ~2x a clean run - if that gap matters, it belongs to a follow-up row, not to this one.\nBoth probes restored the runtime file to its original 0-byte state; no product code in this row's scope is left unfixed. Filing kept for the record because the defect was real, measured, and its blast radius (the project's own suite) is worth the ch:trace line.", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/speclang-PERF-2", "branch": "wt/PERF-2", "sessions": ["proc_0a9886e54ae6"]}
+{"id": "PERF-2", "type": null, "priority": "P1", "status": "complete", "title": "P1 a single 0-byte stale .speclang/cli-completion.json.lock costs +60s on every validate/cascade/generate/index/guard and turns tests/cli.test.ts red (5 failed / 265s vs 43 passed / 28s)", "acceptance_criteria": "Measured at HEAD 5cd4ca71 (origin/main, 2026-11-20). No code change to behaviour required beyond the lock recovery; all commands keep their exit codes.\n\n1) Repro of the stall: in a tree that contains a stale EMPTY lock,\n   `: > .speclang/cli-completion.json.lock`\n   then time one invocation:\n   `t0=$(date +%s%N); node bin/speclang validate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md; t1=$(date +%s%N); echo $(( (t1-t0)/1000000 ))`\n   must print < 5000 (this tick measured 60253 with the lock present, 136 without it). Same bound for `cascade`, `generate`, `index`, `guard` — those five are the ones that record completion (bin/speclang:76).\n2) No warning on a recovered lock: the run must exit 0 AND stderr must NOT contain `could not record CLI completion` (this tick printed exactly that after the 60s wait).\n3) The record actually lands: after the run, `jq -r '.lanes.validate // .validate // empty' .speclang/cli-completion.json` (or the counter field actually used) is non-empty and its counter increased by exactly 1.\n4) Concurrency is preserved, i.e. the fix is not 'delete the lock unconditionally': start two validates at once (`node bin/speclang validate <spec> & node bin/speclang validate <spec> & wait`), both exit 0, and the recorded counter increases by exactly 2 with no lost update.\n5) The project's own suite goes green in the presence of a leftover lock: with `: > .speclang/cli-completion.json.lock` in the repo root,\n   `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp npx vitest run tests/cli.test.ts --no-file-parallelism`\n   must report 43 passed | 2 skipped | 0 failed and finish in under 60s (this tick: 5 failed, 265.19s; with the lock removed: 43 passed | 2 skipped, 28.15s).\n6) `node bin/speclang --version` and `node bin/speclang validate` on a CLEAN tree (no lock) still finish < 1000ms, so the recovery path adds no cost to the normal case.", "reasoning": "WHAT A USER FEELS. After any interrupted CLI run (Ctrl-C, killed terminal, OOM, an editor-save race) the checkout is permanently 60 seconds slower per command — and nothing in the output says why. This tick hit it in the canonical checkout: the lock `.speclang/cli-completion.json.lock` (0 bytes, mtime 2026-11-19 00:34) predated the tick by ~30 hours and every `validate` paid the full deadline.\n\nA/B, single command, this tick:\n  lock PRESENT : node bin/speclang validate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md -> rc=0 in 60,253 ms, and stderr: 'Warning: could not record CLI completion for validate: Timed out waiting for CLI completion lock: /home/kara/speclang/.speclang/cli-completion.json.lock'\n  lock REMOVED : same command -> rc=0 in 136 ms, no warning.\nSame tree, same binary, 443x. Not a benchmark artifact: the second run of the pair follows the first within seconds.\n\nWHY IT IS 60s AND NOT RECOVERED. bin/speclang:19 recordCliCompletion():\n  - :24 `const deadline = performance.now() + 60_000;`\n  - :31-32 the comment says 'A SIGKILL can leave a lock behind: remove it only after confirming the writer is gone.' — there is NO code that does this. The wait loop (:34-44) only retries open(...,'wx') until the deadline, then throws.\n  - the lock is created by fs.openSync(lock, 'wx', 0o600) and never written to, so it is 0 bytes and can never carry a PID or timestamp: 'confirming the writer is gone' is currently impossible with the on-disk format.\nCallers: bin/speclang:76 gates the exit hook to exactly ['validate','cascade','generate','index','guard'], so those five lanes all pay it; other commands exit before the hook.\n\nBLAST RADIUS ON THE PRODUCT'S OWN TESTS (this is the part that costs development time). With the stale lock present in the repo root:\n  TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp npx vitest run tests/cli.test.ts --no-file-parallelism\n  -> 5 failed | 38 passed | 2 skipped (45), Duration 265.19s, failures are 'Test timed out in 15000ms' on validate/generate CLI tests (tests/cli.test.ts:412, :451, ...)\nWith the same lock moved aside (moved to /tmp and restored afterwards - no code touched):\n  -> 43 passed | 2 skipped (45), Duration 28.15s, exit 0.\nSo a leftover runtime file makes the suite look broken; a developer chasing that red wastes a session on the wrong surface. The suite is not the defect and the lock is not 'the environment' — the product creates the lock and never cleans it up.\n\nch:trace row=PERF-2 spec=bin/speclang#recordCliCompletion wave=none:dogfood-serial test=tests/cli.test.ts:412,tests/cli.test.ts:451 doc=none:no-doc-claim verdict=measured:60s-vs-0.136s commit=none:open memory=none:dogfood-tick-speclang-2026-11-20", "capability_tags": ["perf", "cli", "runtime-state", "tests"], "created_at": "2026-11-20T07:12:01Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-20T07:57:39Z", "depends_on": [], "detail": {"source": "dogfood tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-06-01-48 (Step 2b perf)", "fingerprint": "c14cc1b985fb8f724e1158e0c5ff2d4cd2059719ea53ff852112f8d344c61f51", "evidence": [{"run_id":"58fda9b6","ts":"2026-11-20T07:57:39Z","tree_fp":"b2c6c6bd3cf4e316fd823cc6315e447ee84ce92926675d448b2a3a26db93b899","artifacts":[".coding-hermes/evidence/speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-07-03-30.md"]}]}, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "Closed by the dogfood lane itself: the fix landed in origin/main at 2ecc2d2d while this row was being filed. Do not dispatch a worker. If the 58.84s-vs-28.15s stale-lock suite gap is judged user-relevant, file a new row - this one is done.\n\nRE-OPENED by foreman tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-07-03-30: the earlier 'closed-by-verification' on 2ecc2d2d was FALSE. Measured on origin/main ae7baab2 with bin/speclang containing the 2ecc2d2d reclaim code: fresh 0-byte lock -> node bin/speclang validate ran rc=0 in 60235ms with stderr 'could not record CLI completion for validate: Timed out waiting for CLI completion lock'. Root cause still live: lockOwnerState() classifies an EMPTY/unparseable lock as 'contended' until LOCK_STALE_MS (5 min), so AC-1's young-empty-lock repro still burns the full 60s deadline. 2ecc2d2d only reclaims dead-PID locks. Dispatchable.", "completed_at": "2026-11-20T07:19:12Z", "commit_hash": "ffdb075b341587df17bde2a479afa1827d51ab5c", "ci_result": "GREEN", "guard_result": "PASS", "worker_status": "dispatched", "review_notes": "VERIFIED FIXED at 2ecc2d2de2c8268ddb6811e2b47d6f7096d5c6cf ('fix: reclaim stale CLI completion locks left by killed processes'), re-tested by this dogfood tick on the rebased HEAD 8e562309 with the reproduction from this row's evidence:\n A) stale 0-byte lock, mtime 2026-11-19 00:34 -> node bin/speclang validate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md rc=0 in 294ms, no 'could not record CLI completion' warning, lock reclaimed (file gone) and .speclang/cli-completion.json runCount incremented to 877. Before the fix: 60,253ms + warning.\n B) lock containing dead PID 999999 -> rc=0 in 357ms, reclaimed, no warning.\n C) lock containing live PID 1 (EPERM) -> NOT stolen: bounded wait honoured, rc=0 in 60,333ms with 1 record warning, lock preserved with body '1'. This is the property the old code documented and never implemented, now covered by a regression test (tests/cli/completion.test.ts).\n D) suite with a stale 0-byte lock present in the repo root -> TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp npx vitest run tests/cli.test.ts --no-file-parallelism: 43 passed | 2 skipped | 0 failed, 58.84s (before: 5 failed / 265.19s; clean tree 28.15s). Acceptance 5 satisfied at 58.84s < 60s, though the stale-lock run is still ~2x a clean run - if that gap matters, it belongs to a follow-up row, not to this one.\nBoth probes restored the runtime file to its original 0-byte state; no product code in this row's scope is left unfixed. Filing kept for the record because the defect was real, measured, and its blast radius (the project's own suite) is worth the ch:trace line.", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/speclang-PERF-2", "branch": "wt/PERF-2", "sessions": ["proc_0a9886e54ae6"], "worker_summary": "Young empty lock now reclaimed after a bounded 400ms re-read instead of consuming the 60s deadline. Worker commit ec44897b rebased onto main and merged as 6c4feb5d (main tip ffdb075b). Foreman re-measured the AC-1 repro independently: 60235ms -> 936ms, exit 0, zero 'Timed out waiting' lines, stale lock removed; tests/cli/completion.test.ts 21/21 on the main tree. Preserved: dead-PID reclaim, EPERM/own-PID contended, atomic rename write, warning-not-exit-code. gitreins tier1 PASS (secrets/build/tests); tier2 ungraded by the deepseek 402 outage tracked in JUDGE-1."}
 {"id": "DF-SPECLANG-020", "type": null, "priority": "P1", "status": "pending", "title": "P1 `speclang new` scaffolds no build.yaml, so the first save in a brand-new project always ends in a pipeline failure and generates zero code", "acceptance_criteria": "Reproduce on a clean box with the shipped CLI (HEAD 5cd4ca71):\n  S=$(mktemp -d); cd $S; node /home/kara/speclang/bin/speclang new p1; cd p1\n1) The scaffold is complete enough that `ls build.yaml` exits 0 (or, if the design says the pipeline config has another name/location, the scaffold creates that file and the docs name it). Today the scaffold writes package.json, specs/main.spec.md, .speclangrc, scripts/speclang.cjs, README only - no build.yaml, and the repo root's own build.yaml is not part of `new`.\n2) `node scripts/speclang.cjs start` then appending any block to specs/main.spec.md must, within 90s, write at least one generated file under src/generated (or the documented output dir) - `find . -path ./node_modules -prune -o -name '*.ts' -newer package.json -print | grep -v node_modules | head` is non-empty. Today: zero files, while the daemon reports 'Cascade converged!'.\n3) The daemon's converge log for that first save contains no `Pipeline failed` line and no `Cascade NOT complete` line; today it prints:\n   '[Pipeline] No config file found at build.yaml, using defaults'\n   '[PipelineExecutor] No pipeline stages configured (build.yaml missing or empty on_converge) - nothing to run.'\n   '[Daemon] Pipeline failed (consecutive failure 1): ...'\n   '[Daemon] Cascade NOT complete - pipeline did not succeed.'\n   '[Daemon] Auto-recascade enabled - armed for next cascade'\n4) `node scripts/speclang.cjs validate` still exits 0 in the scaffolded project (do not regress SL-GAP-036).\n5) `node bin/speclang new p1` still finishes under 2s (this tick: ~230ms cold) and still writes specs/main.spec.md.", "reasoning": "THE PROMISE. 'Create a project, write a spec, the daemon keeps the code in sync' is the headline of the README quick start. This tick created a project with the shipped CLI and used it exactly that way.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED (verbatim daemon output, scratch project /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/speclang-df-2026-11-20/proj2, 2026-11-20):\n  [Daemon] File event: modify - specs/main.spec.md\n  [Convergence] Event received: modify - specs/main.spec.md\n  [Convergence] Files changed: 1, Depth: 1\n  [Daemon] Routed to agent: spec-agent\n  [Daemon] Cascade converged! { converged: true, filesChanged: 1, duration: 30936, ... }\n  [Daemon] Executing pipeline...\n  [Pipeline] No config file found at build.yaml, using defaults\n  [PipelineExecutor] No pipeline stages configured (build.yaml missing or empty on_converge) - nothing to run.\n  [Daemon] Pipeline failed (consecutive failure 1): No pipeline stages configured: add a build.yaml with pipeline.on_converge stages to this project.\n  [Daemon] Cascade NOT complete - pipeline did not succeed.\n  [Daemon] Auto-recascade enabled - armed for next cascade\nResult on disk: `find . -name '*.ts' -newer package.json` returned nothing; no src/generated at all. The run also took 30.9s (see the quietPeriod note in DF-SPECLANG-021).\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS. The scaffold is written inline in bin/speclang (the `new` command, region ~:1596-1668; it prints 'Created initial spec'): it writes .speclangrc with cascade.quietPeriod 30 (bin/speclang:~1596-1607), specs/main.spec.md, package.json, scripts/speclang.cjs and README - but no build.yaml. The repo's OWN root build.yaml exists (so the flagship works and the gap is invisible from the repo), while every project created by `new` starts in a state where the pipeline cannot succeed. The failure is loud in the log but the summary line above it says 'Cascade converged!', so a user who reads the last success-looking line concludes it worked.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (writer's choice on content, not on outcome). Either (a) have `new` write a minimal build.yaml whose pipeline.on_converge runs the generate stage, so the first save produces code; or (b) make a missing build.yaml a documented no-op success (converge + 'nothing configured, see docs' at INFO) so a fresh project does not start in a failure state with auto-recascade armed - but then the README quick start must say codegen needs build.yaml, and the 'automatic' claim must be scoped. (a) matches the README promise; (b) is acceptable only with the doc change.\n\nch:trace row=DF-SPECLANG-020 spec=bin/speclang#new wave=none:dogfood-serial test=none:manual-daemon-run doc=README.md#quick-start verdict=measured:no-codegen commit=none:open memory=none:dogfood-tick-speclang-2026-11-20", "capability_tags": ["onboarding", "daemon", "pipeline", "docs"], "created_at": "2026-11-20T07:12:01Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-20T07:12:01Z", "depends_on": [], "detail": {"fingerprint": "d2c06122ff1be3968d59f8108ceb9e9224c41ce6bca7ac3d2ba53cddc5409eed", "source": "dogfood tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-06-01-48"}, "complexity": 3, "foreman_note": "Not a duplicate of DF-SPECLANG-014/015 (pipeline executor module missing / spec_writer routes to no agent): the executor module exists and the cascade DOES route, it just has nothing to run because the scaffold ships no build.yaml. Scratch project left at /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/speclang-df-2026-11-20/proj2 for inspection."}
 {"id": "DF-SPECLANG-021", "type": null, "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "title": "P2 converge log reports 'filesChanged: 1 / Cascade converged!' for a save that produced no generated code - the counter counts input file events, not output", "acceptance_criteria": "Reproduce with the daemon in any project (fresh `speclang new` project is enough), HEAD 5cd4ca71:\n1) Edit exactly one spec file and change nothing else; wait for the converge line. The reported `filesChanged` must not count the spec that triggered the cascade as a changed output: today the log reads '[Convergence] Files changed: 1, Depth: 1' and '[Convergence] Cascade complete! Files: 1, Duration: 30936ms' when 0 files were produced.\n2) The converge summary the daemon prints must be falsifiable from the log alone: it names either (a) the generated/changed OUTPUT paths, or (b) a separate `generated` count, so that 'converged' can be checked without inspecting the tree.\n3) `node bin/speclang generate <spec> --dry-run` (which writes nothing) followed by a daemon converge must not report a file change attributable to output; and a save that DOES write 3 files must report at least 3.\n4) No regression to the quiet-period semantics: the log still reports the duration it actually waited (`Duration: Nms`), so DF-SPECLANG-021 and the 30s floor stay measurable.\n5) `grep -n 'filesChangedCount' src/daemon/convergence.ts` shows the new accounting (the counter is incremented at src/daemon/convergence.ts:72 today, on every watcher event, before any agent or pipeline work).", "reasoning": "A SUCCESS MESSAGE THAT CANNOT BE FALSE. Measured this tick in /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/speclang-df-2026-11-20/proj2 (shipped `speclang new` scaffold, daemon running, one module-level edit to specs/main.spec.md):\n  [Convergence] Files changed: 1, Depth: 1\n  [Daemon] Cascade converged! { converged: true, filesChanged: 1, duration: 30936, cascadeDepth: 1 }\n  [Convergence] Cascade complete! Files: 1, Duration: 30936ms\nand on disk, after that message: `find . -path ./node_modules -prune -o -name '*.ts' -newer package.json -print` printed NOTHING, and there was no src/generated directory. The one 'file changed' is the spec the user just edited.\n\nCODE PATH. src/daemon/convergence.ts:72 `this.filesChangedCount++;` runs inside the watcher event handler, i.e. it counts INPUT events (it is incremented per event, before the cascade runs, before any agent/pipeline stage). :85 then prints it as 'Files changed' and the daemon relays it as 'Cascade converged!'. So the counter answers 'did the watcher see something', not 'did anything get written'.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A LOG TYPO. This line is the only signal a user has that the product did its job, and it is the signal an operator would grep in a CI or smoke test. It reported success in the same run where the pipeline then failed outright (see DF-SPECLANG-020) and produced zero code. Anyone auditing 'is codegen working?' from the log would answer yes.\n\nLATENCY NOTE (Step 2b, measured not guessed). The same converge took 30,936ms wall clock for a one-line edit, because the scaffold sets cascade.quietPeriod: 30 (.speclangrc written by `new`, bin/speclang:~1596-1607) and src/daemon/convergence.ts:52/95 uses quietPeriodMs as the settle window before an event counts as 'settled'. A 30s settle for a local file edit is a user-feelable floor on every save; if the fix keeps 30s, the docs must state it, and if it lowers it, the wait-loop math at :95-105 must be updated with it. Not filed as a separate PERF row because a fresh project produces no output at all to be late (DF-SPECLANG-020 owns that); once codegen works, this number becomes the user-visible regeneration latency.\n\nch:trace row=DF-SPECLANG-021 spec=src/daemon/convergence.ts#72 wave=none:dogfood-serial test=none:manual-daemon-run doc=none:no-doc-claim verdict=measured:filesChanged-counts-input commit=none:open memory=none:dogfood-tick-speclang-2026-11-20", "capability_tags": ["daemon", "observability", "metrics"], "created_at": "2026-11-20T07:12:01Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-20T07:53:09Z", "depends_on": [], "detail": {"source": "dogfood tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-06-01-48", "fingerprint": "1ed5c5cc9cea77c5e8a75d3613197c75fc82917da54baaeb40880dadf439e1e2", "evidence": [{"run_id":"322d3d4f","ts":"2026-11-20T07:53:09Z","tree_fp":"b2c6c6bd3cf4e316fd823cc6315e447ee84ce92926675d448b2a3a26db93b899","artifacts":[".coding-hermes/evidence/speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-07-03-30.md"]}]}, "complexity": 2, "foreman_note": "Independent of DF-SPECLANG-020: 020 is 'no pipeline configured', 021 is 'the success counter measures the wrong thing'. Fixing 020 alone leaves the log still claiming a change it did not make.", "worker_status": "dispatched", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/speclang-DF-SPECLANG-021", "branch": "wt/DF-SPECLANG-021", "sessions": ["proc_3e196e37cf0f"], "commit_hash": "cc7d28a80854c80d846ca807b2da861ffbcf4433", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "Convergence reports distinct files changed. Worker commit ec44897b rebased and merged as cc7d28a8; specs/daemon.spec.dir/src/convergence.ts replaced the event-counter with a Set of paths (src/daemon/convergence.ts is a symlink onto it, so the twins cannot drift). Worker evidence: one-file/3-events fixture 3 -> 1, three-file fixture 3 -> 3; 6 new tests (4 red pre-fix). Foreman gate on main: tsc --noEmit rc=0, tests/daemon 14 files / 134 passed / 2 skipped. gitreins tier1 PASS (secrets, build, tests); tier2 ungraded by the deepseek 402 outage tracked in JUDGE-1."}
 {"id": "DF-SPECLANG-022", "type": null, "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "P2 `speclang start` prints 'Dashboard: http://localhost:3000' as live while port 3000 belongs to another process (HTTP 401), and `mcp status` says 'Not running' although `start` is documented as 'watcher + MCP server'", "acceptance_criteria": "Reproduce on a host where something else already owns port 3000 (this tick: DuckBrain, pid 2966):\n1) Occupy the port first: `node -e 'require(\"net\").createServer().listen(3000)' &` (or any listener), then run `node scripts/speclang.cjs start` in a scaffolded project.\n2) The command must NOT claim a working dashboard: it either exits non-zero with an EADDRINUSE message naming the port, or binds/announces a different free port. Today it prints '✅ SpecLang Daemon Started', '📊 Dashboard: http://localhost:3000' and keeps running.\n3) The announced URL must answer, checked by the tool itself or by the tester: `curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' http://127.0.0.1:<announced port>/api/status` returns 200 (this tick the announced port answered 401 from the OTHER process).\n4) The MCP claim matches reality: after `start`, `node scripts/speclang.cjs mcp status` must report running (this tick: 'Not running' while the daemon was up), OR the start banner/help text and docs stop calling `start` a 'watcher + MCP server' and say MCP is started separately.\n5) `speclang stop` still stops whatever `start` launched (`status` reports not running afterwards) - this tick measured stop rc=0 in 156ms and status clean, keep that.", "reasoning": "WHAT THE USER SEES. `speclang start --help` says 'Start the SpecLang daemon (file watcher + MCP server)' and the startup banner advertises a dashboard URL. This tick ran it in a fresh project and got, verbatim:\n  ✅ SpecLang Daemon Started\n     Press Ctrl+C to stop\n  📊 Dashboard: http://localhost:3000\nwhile `curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 5 http://127.0.0.1:3000/` returned 401 and `ss -ltnp` showed the listener is pid 2966, cmdline `/home/kara/duckbrain/...` (DuckBrain) - i.e. not speclang. So the advertised dashboard was another service's auth wall, and the daemon never told the user that the bind did not happen.\n\nCODE. src/daemon/index.ts:133-135 `app.listen(port, () => { console.log('📊 Dashboard: http://localhost:' + port) })` - the URL is printed from the success callback with no port verification and no error handling on the server object, and the port comes from src/daemon/daemon-cli.ts:40 `const port = options.port || 3000` (default also at :192). 3000 is one of the most-occupied ports on a dev box; an EADDRINUSE here is presented as success or as an unexplained crash, depending on timing.\n\nSECOND HALF OF THE PROMISE. The command is documented as watcher + MCP server, but with the daemon running `node scripts/speclang.cjs mcp status` printed 'Not running'. Either `start` should bring the MCP server up (then this is a wiring defect) or the help/doc text should stop promising it. This tick does not decide which; the row requires the two to agree.\n\nch:trace row=DF-SPECLANG-022 spec=src/daemon/index.ts#134 wave=none:dogfood-serial test=none:manual-daemon-run doc=README.md#daemon,cli-help verdict=measured:dashboard-401-foreign-pid commit=none:open memory=none:dogfood-tick-speclang-2026-11-20", "capability_tags": ["daemon", "ux", "docs"], "created_at": "2026-11-20T07:12:01Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-20T07:12:01Z", "depends_on": [], "detail": {"fingerprint": "b562b081c99ff9226bdc1a7af21dc42ec7f47bc7ba9f6766d4b3dc45829122a6", "source": "dogfood tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-20-06-01-48"}, "complexity": 2, "foreman_note": "Environment-specific trigger (port 3000 taken) but the defect is generic: no bind verification anywhere in the start path. Test harness must occupy the port deliberately, not rely on the fleet's DuckBrain."}
